\begin{abstract}
Active-learning studies bound achievable performance with oracles that select labels in hindsight, and report the oracle's lead over practical strategies as headroom. We show that, when outcomes are noisy, much of this lead is unreachable. We split the oracle gap into test-set reuse, an aleatoric floor created by the randomness of the acquired labels, and a deployable gap, and estimate all three on real data with a probability model fitted on disjoint records. Across eight credit, readmission, marketing, e-mail and digit cohorts and three learners (logistic, boosting and a neural network), the floor is material in nearly every cohort and its share grows with label noise: on the raw-pixel digit task used by a recent benchmark the oracle's lead is mostly real, whereas on noisy credit and readmission outcomes 58--100\% of it is test reuse and floor. A closed-form bracket built from near-tied leading sets contains the measured floor in all 87 settings we examined, and under a risk budget the floor rises with the budget in every cohort. Which strategy closes the remaining deployable gap depends on whether its information proxy matches the learner; a risk-conservative policy, DC-PRISM, illustrates both outcomes.
\keywords{Active learning \and Evaluation \and Oracle bounds \and Aleatoric uncertainty \and Risk-constrained acquisition}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
How much better could an acquisition strategy be? Active-learning research increasingly answers with an \emph{oracle}: a selector that, unlike any deployable strategy, may look at the labels it acquires and at the evaluation set, and so traces the best attainable learning curve. Recent benchmarks construct greedy oracles that add, step by step, the candidate whose \emph{realized} label most improves test performance~\cite{werner2023comparable,werner2024cdalbench}, and best-of-strategies oracles that pick, per batch, the strategy whose labelled batch helps most~\cite{huseljic2026boss}. The gap between oracle and practical strategies is then read as headroom: state-of-the-art strategies are reported to ``fall noticeably short of oracle performance, especially in large-scale datasets''~\cite{huseljic2026boss}. This complements a broader effort to make active-learning evaluation reliable~\cite{luth2023navigating,ji2023randomness}.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Decomposition and estimator.} We split an oracle's reported lead into test-set reuse, an \emph{aleatoric floor} that no deployable strategy can close, and a \emph{deployable gap}, and estimate all three on real data (Fig.~\ref{fig:framework}) by resampling hidden labels from a probability model fitted on disjoint records. The estimator is validated against known probabilities and calibrated against model-free realized outcomes (Secs.~\ref{sec:protocol},~\ref{sec:validation}).
\item \textbf{A predictable floor.} The floor is set by label noise between near-tied leading sets: a closed-form bracket contains it in all 87 settings we examined, its share of the oracle gap rises with label noise across eight cohorts as we predicted before running, and under a risk budget it grows with the budget in every cohort (Sec.~\ref{sec:findings}).
\item \textbf{What remains.} On low-noise benchmark data the oracle's lead is mostly real; on noisy outcomes 58--100\% of it is reuse and floor. Who closes the deployable remainder depends on whether a strategy's information proxy matches the learner; DC-PRISM, a conservative policy for risk-constrained acquisition, wins on one domain and loses on another, and a well-powered delayed replay confirms the ordering (Secs.~\ref{sec:findings},~\ref{sec:replay}).
\end{itemize}

