\section{Outcome Check: Delayed Replay}
\label{sec:replay}
The protocol scores single rounds. To check its ordering under dynamics we replayed ten rounds (exploration labels after one round, ordinary labels after two; ALC-Brier averages ten round-start scores) with 512 fresh seeds per credit cell and 256 per readmission stream, numbers registered from the variance of an earlier batch. Table~\ref{tab:replay} shows the ordering the protocol predicts. Where information-greedy rules captured the deployable gap and DC-PRISM did not (engineered HMEQ and LendingClub), DC-PRISM is materially worse than Fisher-cost, BAIT-greedy and TargetVar-greedy ($2.5$--$3.8$); where its gain over the quantity reference was significant, it beats Safe-MinRisk and fixed count ($2.2$--$3.1$); on engineered readmission it beats BAIT-greedy and TargetVar-greedy ($1.3$, $1.4$); and where little deployable gap exists (natural streams, engineered UCI) no comparison is material. Realized adverse exposure exceeded the envelope in 0--4 of 40{,}960 acquisition rounds per credit policy and in none of 5{,}120 for readmission.

