\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
\paragraph{Evaluating active learning.} Recent work shows that active-learning gains are fragile under realistic evaluation~\cite{luth2023navigating}, sensitive to uncontrolled randomness~\cite{ji2023randomness} and inconsistent across domains~\cite{werner2023comparable,werner2024cdalbench}, and uses oracles to bound attainable performance~\cite{werner2024cdalbench,huseljic2026boss}. Werner et al.\ note that their greedy oracle overfits the test set~\cite{werner2023comparable}. We quantify that reuse and add an aleatoric component that remains after it is removed.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
An oracle's lead is a natural yardstick for how much an acquisition strategy could improve. Our decomposition shows that the yardstick carries an aleatoric floor, set by label noise among near-tied leading sets, together with test reuse. The floor is predictable in closed form, rises with outcome noise and with the risk budget, and depends on the learner. On low-noise benchmark data the oracle's lead is mostly real; on noisy outcomes most of it is not, and what remains is captured only by strategies whose information proxy matches the learner. We recommend reporting oracle gaps on held-out evaluation data, together with the floor bracket~\eqref{eq:two} or the decomposition when outcome probabilities can be estimated from disjoint records.

